.\" Hand-written on purpose, and it names no flag but --help -- see the note at
.\" the top of issuerd.8 for why, and `make test` for what holds it there.
.TH KERBRIDGE-SYNC 8 "KerBridge" "KerBridge" "System Manager's Manual"
.SH NAME
kerbridge-sync \- KerBridge directory synchronisation daemon
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B kerbridge-sync
mirrors the admitted users and their groups out of a cloud identity provider
into this realm's Samba AD directory. It reads the provider over HTTPS and
writes the directory over LDAPS, in cycles, at a configured interval.
.PP
The direction is one-way and the cloud directory is never written. Each source
owns exactly one organisational unit here, and reconciliation is scoped to it:
accounts are created inside it, renames are followed, and a group that no
longer exists upstream is retired rather than deleted, so that its security
identifier \- and every filesystem access control entry carrying it \- survives.
.PP
It is the only KerBridge daemon that changes durable state in the directory,
which is why it keeps an audit record of its own beside the service log. Your
own resource groups live outside every synchronised organisational unit
precisely so that this daemon does not own them and cannot reconcile them away;
.BR kbmanage (1)
is what manages those.
.SH CONFIGURATION
.B kerbridge-sync
reads the KerBridge configuration set, a directory of TOML files whose default
location is
.IR /etc/kerbridge .
Its own file there is
.IR sync.toml ,
and it reads
.IR realm.toml ,
.I main.toml
and one
.I idp_<source>.toml
per identity provider beside it.
.PP
The set is validated before the daemon starts, by
.B kbconfig check
as
.B ExecStartPre
of the unit below. A set that does not check out keeps this one service from
starting, and affects nothing else; systemd retries five times and then leaves
the unit
.BR failed ,
so the reason stays in
.B systemctl status
instead of scrolling past in the journal.
.PP
With no configuration set at all the unit is skipped rather than started:
.B ConditionPathExists=/etc/kerbridge/main.toml
guards it, so an installed-but-unconfigured host is quiet and
.B systemctl start
still exits 0.
.PP
No credential is ever written in the configuration set. Secrets are files under
.IR /etc/kerbridge.secrets/ ,
named in the set as paths and read through the
.B _kerbridge
group.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/kerbridge/sync.toml
This daemon's own configuration.
.TP
.I /var/log/kerbridge/sync/audit.log
The append-only record of what each cycle applied and what it could not.
Rotated by
.IR /etc/logrotate.d/kerbridge-sync ,
which signals the daemon to reopen the file rather than truncating it under its
feet.
.TP
.I /var/lib/kerbridge/sync/
Durable state: the operator-notification conditions currently outstanding, and
the synchronisation cursor.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGUSR1
Reopen the audit log. Sent by
.BR logrotate (8)
after it has rotated the file.
.SH OPTIONS
kerbridge-sync takes a small and stable set of options, and this page deliberately
names none of them: a hand-written list is a copy, and a copy is what goes
stale the first time an option changes. Run
.B kerbridge-sync --help
for the set this build has. An argument it does not recognise is refused rather
than ignored, and the refusal prints the same usage line.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
